    "reading": {
        "body": "Colorado's 2026 legislative session produced 32 bills, and the ones that touch AI split into two groups: rules for how AI is used, and rules for the data centers that run it.\n\nThe usage bills cover health care, psychotherapy, conversational AI operators, automated decision-making, and age attestation on devices. That is a state writing sector-by-sector AI rules rather than one comprehensive statute. The infrastructure bills are the more unusual signal: SB102 on large-load data centers, HB1030 on data center and utility modernization, HB1326 sunsetting the Public Utilities Commission, and HB1246 on consumer-regulated electric utilities. A state legislature taking up large-load data centers and utility modernization in the same session is treating compute buildout as a grid and siting question, not just a technology question. The local layer reinforces it, with a September news item on a city move to prohibit AI data centers.\n\nWhat the data does not show is outcome. These are bill records with dates, not enacted law, and nothing here reports votes, amendments, or vetoes. It also does not quantify anything about Colorado's power supply, interconnection queues, or how much load any proposed data center would draw. The count of 32 covers only what this dataset captured, and the news coverage is three items, too thin to establish a trend on its own.",
